<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What the FileProvider is allowed to expose.

  Exactly one directory: the one holding verified, complete files. Never the whole cache, never
  external storage, never the in-flight staging directory. A FileProvider rooted at "." would let
  any path traversal that got past the filename sanitiser turn into a readable URI for arbitrary
  app-private data, so the root is kept as narrow as the feature allows.

  The path must be the FULL path under cacheDir, including the "relay_cache" container:
  ContentCache puts files in <cacheDir>/relay_cache/ready/. This said "ready/" for a while, which
  resolves to <cacheDir>/ready/ and matches nothing, so the first real getUriForFile call threw
  IllegalArgumentException and took the app down. It went unnoticed because nothing exercised the
  provider until the received-files actions existed. Keep it in step with
  ContentCache.DIRECTORY_NAME; FileProviderPathTest fails if it drifts again.

  "incoming/" is deliberately absent. Partials are unverified: their hash has not been checked, so
  their bytes are whatever the peer sent. Granting a URI to one would hand another app a
  half-written file that failed integrity checking, and nothing in the app needs to read a partial
  back out through a content URI: the receiver holds its own File handle while writing.
-->
<paths>
    <!-- Verified, complete files, ready to open or share. -->
    <cache-path name="received" path="relay_cache/ready/" />
</paths>
